{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-976c27b43f806d61","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9018-E001\n3\ttitle: Per-project GPU-hour consumption reporting with stable API and CSV export\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n5\tcomponent: Distributed Workloads\n6\tteam: Distributed Workloads\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tai_signals:\n10\t  change_specificity: 0\n11\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n12\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n13\t  existing_foundation: 0\n14\t  open_questions: 0\n15\t  external_dependency: -1\n16\t  human_process_gates: 1\n17\t  repo_access: 1\n18\t  architecture_claims: 1\n19\timplementation_type: null\n20\tdependencies: []\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: Medium\n23\tai_implementability_score: 2\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Per-project GPU-hour consumption reporting with stable API and CSV export\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers the core administrator-facing chargeback reporting\n34\tcapability: per-project GPU-hour consumption reports computed over selectable\n35\ttime ranges from the per-project usage data delivered by the in-flight GPU\n36\tusage metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990). Reports are exposed through a stable\n37\tAPI for finance tooling and exportable as CSV. This epic consumes the metering\n38\tdata; it does not re-implement the metering collection pipeline.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Compute per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges,\n43\t  reading the per-project GPU-hour usage data delivered by the GPU usage\n44\t  metering strategy.\n45\t- Expose the reports through a stable, documented API suitable for finance\n46\t  tooling retrieval.\n47\t- Provide CSV export of the reports.\n48\t- Define and apply consistent GPU-hour aggregation semantics (what counts as a\n49\t  GPU-hour, how usage aggregates over the selected time range) across the API\n50\t  and CSV outputs.\n51\t- Reside in the Distributed Workloads stack: the Dashboard owns no data plane,\n52\t  so the report computation and delivery surface belong to this component.\n53\t\n54\tOut of scope: the metering collection pipeline itself (owned by the metering\n55\tstrategy), and currency/cost-rate modelling — reports are in GPU-hours, not\n56\tdollars.\n57\t\n58\t## Acceptance Criteria\n59\t\n60\t- Given metered per-project GPU-hour usage data, an administrator can retrieve\n61\t  a per-project consumption report for a selectable time range, and the\n62\t  reported totals reconcile with the underlying metering data for that range.\n63\t- Reports are retrievable via a stable, documented API and are exportable as\n64\t  CSV, with both delivery modes producing consistent figures.\n65\t- The report implementation consumes the metering data as delivered by the\n66\t  metering strategy and, once that strategy lands its final data schema, is\n67\t  validated against the final schema — any divergence is reconciled in the\n68\t  report implementation without re-implementing collection.\n69\t- The GPU-hour aggregation semantics used by the reports are implemented\n70\t  deterministically and identically across the API and CSV outputs.\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability\n73\t\n74\t- **HLR-1 (P0)**: per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable\n75\t  time ranges computed from the metering data — fully covered by this epic.\n76\t- **HLR-2 (P1)**: CSV export and stable API retrieval for finance tooling —\n77\t  covered by this epic. Bundled with the P0 reporting work rather than split:\n78\t  export and API retrieval are delivery modes of the same report surface over\n79\t  the same per-project consumption data structures, so the HLR is not\n80\t  independently deferrable and splitting it would build the same surface twice.\n81\t\n82\t## Risks\n83\t\n84\t- The metering data schema is owned by the metering strategy and finalizes only\n85\t  when that strategy lands; report field mappings and aggregation logic carry\n86\t  rework risk until then. Mitigated by the schema-reconciliation acceptance\n87\t  criterion above; implementation may proceed against the schema as currently\n88\t  delivered.\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9f058cad5b62c3e","agent_id":"a048b9d89164af80a","session_id":"0a40a135-68eb-4401-87dc-e3c142162fc5","uuid":"50016aad-75ab-409d-82ed-6c5f934ba67f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:22:42.478Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-976c27b43f806d61","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
