{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3d310cf3c14f15a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy: GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators (Major, Refinement\n13\tDone). Builds administrator-facing per-project GPU-hour consumption reporting on\n14\ttop of the per-project usage data delivered by the in-flight metering strategy\n15\t(RHAISTRAT-9990), without re-implementing collection.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n20\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reporting with stable API and CSV export | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Dashboard admin view of current-month GPU consumption against Kueue quota | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | Documentation | P1 |\n24\t\n25\t## Component and Team Mapping\n26\t\n27\t- **E001 → Distributed Workloads**: the strategy lists Dashboard and Distributed\n28\t  Workloads as affected components. The Dashboard \"owns no data plane\" and\n29\t  speaks the Kubernetes API only (per its architecture-context file), so the\n30\t  report computation and API cannot live in the Dashboard component; the\n31\t  Distributed Workloads stack (Kueue quota model, usage metrics) is the\n32\t  canonical home for the reporting surface. Both `Distributed Workloads` and\n33\t  `Dashboard` are verbatim canonical component names from\n34\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n35\t- **E003 → Documentation**: not listed in the strategy's Affected Components,\n36\t  but HLR-4 is substantive documentation authoring (new doc topics on report\n37\t  semantics and export integration), which warrants a separate\n38\t  `docs-authoring` epic under the canonical `Documentation` component.\n39\t- Owner teams are absent from the strategy (see Health Warnings) and were\n40\t  inferred from architecture context: Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard;\n41\t  Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads.\n42\t\n43\t## Dependency DAG\n44\t\n45\t```mermaid\n46\tgraph TD\n47\t    E001[\"E001 Report computation, stable API, CSV export\"] --> E002[\"E002 Dashboard admin view\"]\n48\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Documentation\"]\n49\t    E002 --> E003\n50\t```\n51\t\n52\t## DAG Justification\n53\t\n54\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n55\t|------|------|-----------|\n56\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 12/13 (artifact producer → consumer) | The Dashboard view builds against the report data surface (per-project consumption figures) produced by the reporting implementation; the consumer must not re-implement the computation, so it is serialized behind the producer. |\n57\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic; documentation describes what was built. |\n58\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation of report semantics and export integration must also cover the administrator view delivered by the Dashboard work. |\n59\t\n60\t**Non-edges (parallel / external):**\n61\t\n62\t- **No epic edge to the metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990).** It is a separate,\n63\t  in-flight strategy owned by another team — an external precondition, not a\n64\t  node in this DAG (Rule 21: third party resolves → precondition, no separate\n65\t  epic). The coupling is carried as an acceptance criterion on E001\n66\t  (reconciliation against the final published schema) and as a health warning.\n67\t- **No onboarding epics.** Both affected components exist in\n68\t  `.context/architecture-context/` and are Konflux-onboarded per their context\n69\t  files, so no repo-onboarding / license-validation / konflux-onboarding chain\n70\t  is generated.\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n73\t\n74\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic | Notes |\n75\t|-----|----------|---------------------------|------|-------|\n76\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges, computed from metering data | RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Core reporting epic. |\n77\t| HLR-2 | P1 | CSV export and stable API retrieval for finance tooling | RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 reporting work: CSV export and API retrieval are delivery modes of the same report surface over the same per-project consumption data structures. HLR-2 is not independently deferrable — splitting it would build the same report/API surface twice and add an artificial serialization edge between two epics sharing one implementation surface. |\n78\t| HLR-3 | P1 | Dashboard admin view of current-month consumption vs Kueue quota | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Distinct component (Dashboard) and team (RHOAI Dashboard) → separate epic per boundary Rule 1. |\n79\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Substantive content authoring (new doc topics) → separate docs-authoring epic. Epic priority is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1), not HLR-4's P2. |\n80\t\n81\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n82\t\n83\t## Health Warnings\n84\t\n85\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n86\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs, and no\n87\t   technical approach or Staff Engineer Input. Decomposition proceeded from the\n88\t   HLR table, affected components, and architecture context; owner teams were\n89\t   inferred from the architecture-context files. Epic acceptance criteria were\n90\t   derived from the HLR wording rather than strategy ACs.\n91\t2. **External timing risk (scope trap watch).** The metering data schema is\n92\t   owned by RHAISTRAT-9990 and is not final until that strategy lands. Report\n93\t   implementation consuming it carries rework risk on field mappings and\n94\t   aggregation logic. This is not a structural unknown (it does not change which\n95\t   epics exist or what they deliver — reporting consumes whatever schema lands),\n96\t   so it is handled as a reconciliation acceptance criterion on E001 plus this\n97\t   warning, not as an Investigation epic.\n98\t3. **Unspecified data-plane surface.** The Dashboard architecture context states\n99\t   the Dashboard owns no data plane and exposes no private component APIs. How\n100\t   report data reaches the Dashboard (e.g., a Kubernetes API surface vs.\n101\t   another mechanism) is an implementation decision for the two teams; this\n102\t   decomposition deliberately does not prescribe it, and E002's dependency on\n103\t   E001 captures only the producer/consumer ordering.\n104\t4. **Docs priority appearance.** E003 is P1 while its HLR-4 is P2 — this is the\n105\t   mandated docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major → P1),\n106\t   not a priority inversion.\n107\t\n108\t## Tiered Delivery\n109\t\n110\tThe single open question is the finalization timing of the metering data schema\n111\t(RHAISTRAT-9990, in flight). Rule 21 applies: the third party (the metering\n112\tstrategy team) resolves it, so it is modeled as a precondition with a tiered\n113\tdelivery evaluation, not as an upstream epic.\n114\t\n115\t**Partial value without the final schema?** Effectively no split is warranted:\n116\t\n117\t- Every HLR (reports, export/API, Dashboard view) consumes per-project GPU-hour\n118\t  usage data; no HLR is deliverable from platform data that is already final\n119\t  (Kueue quota alone does not satisfy HLR-3).\n120\t- The metering pipeline is already delivering per-project GPU-hour data in\n121\t  flight — only schema finalization is pending. Reporting work can proceed\n122\t  against the schema as delivered, so there is no independently schedulable\n123\t  Tier 1 / Tier 2 epic split; the residual risk is fix-and-rework within E001,\n124\t  captured as a schema-reconciliation acceptance criterion and Health Warning 2.\n125\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a62252639080d96e","agent_id":"aef3493fde2dfd87e","session_id":"0a40a135-68eb-4401-87dc-e3c142162fc5","uuid":"71be5e98-ff71-4cc4-89f7-cf38c24e29e9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:25:55.928Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3d310cf3c14f15a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
